<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="notes-week-07">
    <title>Module 7: Vector Spaces and Bases</title>

    <objectives>

      <p>After completing this module, you should be able to:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><emphasis role="bold">VSB1</emphasis> Determine whether a given set satisfies the definition of a vector space (or is a subspace).</li>
        <li><emphasis role="bold">VSB2</emphasis> Find a basis for an abstract vector space.</li>
        <li><emphasis role="bold">VSB3</emphasis> Translate between coordinate systems for different bases.</li>
        <li><emphasis role="bold">VSB4</emphasis> Find a basis for the null space of a given matrix.</li>
        <li><emphasis role="bold">VSB5</emphasis> Find a basis for the column space of a given matrix.</li>
      </ul>
    </objectives>
<introduction>
<p>
For more details on this material, see
<url href="https://ximera.osu.edu/oerlinalg/LinearAlgebra/XLAChapter_subspacesRn/main">Linear Algebra: An Interactive Introduction (Subspaces of <m>\mathbb{R}^n</m> Chapter)</url> and
<url href="https://ximera.osu.edu/oerlinalg/LinearAlgebra/XLAChapter_vecSpaces/main">Linear Algebra: An Interactive Introduction (Vector Spaces Chapter)</url> and.
</p>
</introduction>
    <subsection>
      <title>Vector Spaces and Subspaces (VSB1)</title>

      <definition>
        <p>
          A <term>vector space</term> over <m>\mathbb{R}</m> is a nonempty set
          <m>V</m> equipped with vector addition and scalar multiplication
          satisfying the following rules for all
          <m>u,v,w\in V</m> and <m>c,d\in\mathbb{R}</m>.
        </p>

        <ol>
          <li><m>u+v\in V</m> (closure)</li>
          <li><m>u+v=v+u</m></li>
          <li><m>(u+v)+w=u+(v+w)</m></li>
          <li>There exists <m>0\in V</m> with <m>u+0=u</m>.</li>
          <li>For each <m>u</m> there exists <m>-u</m> with <m>u+(-u)=0</m>.</li>
          <li><m>cu\in V</m> (closure)</li>
          <li><m>c(u+v)=cu+cv</m></li>
          <li><m>(c+d)u=cu+du</m></li>
          <li><m>c(du)=(cd)u</m></li>
          <li><m>1u=u</m></li>
        </ol>
      </definition>

      <remark>
        <p>
          Useful identities that always hold in a vector space:
          <m>(-1)u=-u</m>,
          <m>0u=0</m>, and
          <m>c0=0</m>.
        </p>
      </remark>

      <subsubsection>
        <title>Common Examples of Vector Spaces</title>

        <ul>
          <li><m>\mathbb{R}^n</m></li>
          <li><m>\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}</m></li>
          <li>The set of all real sequences</li>
          <li>The set of all real-valued functions on a fixed domain</li>
          <li>The polynomial space <m>P_n</m></li>
        </ul>
      </subsubsection>

      <example>
        <title>Showing that <m>P_n</m> is a Vector Space</title>

        <p>
          Let
          <m>p(x)=a_0+a_1x+\cdots+a_nx^n</m>
          and
          <m>q(x)=b_0+b_1x+\cdots+b_nx^n</m>.
        </p>

        <me>
        p(x)+q(x)
        =(a_0+b_0)+(a_1+b_1)x+\cdots+(a_n+b_n)x^n
        \in P_n.
        </me>

        <p>For <m>c\in\mathbb R</m>,</p>

        <me>
        cp(x)
        =(ca_0)+(ca_1)x+\cdots+(ca_n)x^n
        \in P_n .
        </me>

        <p>
          The remaining vector-space properties are inherited from the real
          numbers. Therefore <m>P_n</m> is a vector space.
        </p>
      </example>

      <subsubsection>
        <title>Subspaces</title>

        <definition>
          <p>
            A subset <m>H\subseteq V</m> is a <term>subspace</term> if:
          </p>

          <ol>
            <li><m>u,v\in H \Rightarrow u+v\in H</m></li>
            <li><m>0\in H</m></li>
            <li>
              <m>u\in H,\; c\in\mathbb R \Rightarrow cu\in H</m>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </definition>

        <example>
          <title>A Subset That Is Not a Subspace</title>

          <p>
            Let
            <m>
            H=\{(a+2b,\,3a-b,\,4):a,b\in\mathbb R\}
            \subset \mathbb R^3.
            </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            Since the third component is always 4, the zero vector
            <m>(0,0,0)</m> is not contained in <m>H</m>. Therefore
            <m>H</m> is not a subspace.
          </p>
        </example>

        <example>
          <title>A Subset That Is a Subspace</title>

          <p>
            Let
            <m>
            K=\{(a+2b,\,3a-b,\,4a):a,b\in\mathbb R\}.
            </m>
          </p>

          <p>
            Closure under addition:
          </p>

          <me>
          u+v=
          ((a+a')+2(b+b'),
          \,3(a+a')-(b+b'),
          \,4(a+a'))
          \in K.
          </me>

          <p>
            Choosing <m>a=b=0</m> gives the zero vector. Scalar
            multiplication is also preserved, so <m>K</m> is a subspace.
          </p>
        </example>

        <remark>
          <p>
            In <m>\mathbb R^3</m>, the span of one nonzero vector is a line
            through the origin, while the span of two non-collinear vectors
            is a plane through the origin.
          </p>
        </remark>
      </subsubsection>
      <theorem xml:id="thm-span-subspace">
    <title>Span is a Subspace</title>

    <statement>
        <p>
            Let
            <m>v_1,\ldots,v_k\in V</m>, a vector space.
            Then
        </p>

        <me>
        \operatorname{span}\{v_1,\ldots,v_k\}
        </me>

        <p>
            is a subspace of
            <m>V</m>.
        </p>
    </statement>

    <proof>
        <p>
            Let
            <m>
            u,w\in
            \operatorname{span}\{v_1,\ldots,v_k\}.
            </m>
        </p>

        <p>
            Then there exist scalars
            <m>a_1,\ldots,a_k</m> and
            <m>b_1,\ldots,b_k</m> such that
        </p>

        <me>
        u=a_1v_1+\cdots+a_kv_k,
        </me>

        <me>
        w=b_1v_1+\cdots+b_kv_k.
        </me>

        <p>
            Therefore
        </p>

        <me>
        u+w
        =
        (a_1+b_1)v_1+\cdots+(a_k+b_k)v_k,
        </me>

        <p>
            so
            <m>u+w</m> is also in the span.
        </p>

        <p>
            If <m>c</m> is a scalar, then
        </p>

        <me>
        cu
        =
        (ca_1)v_1+\cdots+(ca_k)v_k,
        </me>

        <p>
            which is also in the span.
        </p>

        <p>
            Finally,
        </p>

        <me>
        0
        =
        0v_1+\cdots+0v_k,
        </me>

        <p>
            so the zero vector belongs to the span.
            Hence
            <m>\operatorname{span}\{v_1,\ldots,v_k\}</m>
            is a subspace of
            <m>V</m>.
        </p>
    </proof>
</theorem>

    </subsection>
<subsection xml:id="null-column-spaces-linear-transforms">
    <title>Null Spaces, Column Spaces, and Linear Transforms</title>

    <objectives>
        <ul>
            <li>Understand the concepts of null space and column space.</li>
            <li>Compare these two notions.</li>
            <li>Connect them to previously learned concepts.</li>
        </ul>
    </objectives>

    <p>
        Recall the homogeneous matrix equation
        <m>Ax=0</m>.
    </p>

    <image xml:id="tikz-nullspace-line" width="50%">
    <latex-image>
    \begin{tikzpicture}

% Domain
\draw[fill=blue!5] (0.5,0) ellipse (2 and 2.5);
\draw[fill=blue!10] (-1,0) -- (1,0.25) -- (1.5,2) -- (-0.5,1.75) -- cycle;
\node at (0.5,-0.2) {$\mathbb R^n$};
\node at (0.5,1.2) {$\operatorname{Null}(A)$};

% sample vectors in kernel
\fill (-0.2,0.3) circle (1.5pt);
\fill (0.7,0.8) circle (1.5pt);
\fill (1.2,1.6) circle (1.5pt);

% codomain
\draw[fill=red!5] (7,0) ellipse (1.25 and 2);
\filldraw[red] (7,1) circle (2pt);
\node[right] at (7,1) {$0\quad\mathbb R^m$};

% arrows
\draw[->,thick] (-0.2,0.3) .. controls (3,0.3) and (5,0.8) .. (7,1);
\draw[->,thick] (0.7,0.8) .. controls (3,1.0) and (5,1.0) .. (7,1);
\draw[->,thick] (1.2,1.6) .. controls (3,1.8) and (5,1.2) .. (7,1);

% transformation label
\node at (3.8,2.3) {$A:\mathbb R^n\to\mathbb R^m$};

\end{tikzpicture}

    </latex-image>
    </image>

    <definition xml:id="def-null-space">
        <statement>
            <p>
                The <term>null space</term> of an
                <m>m\times n</m> matrix <m>A</m>,
                denoted by <m>\operatorname{Null}(A)</m> or
                <m>\ker(A)</m>,
                is the set of all vectors <m>x</m> satisfying
                <m>Ax=0</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <example xml:id="ex-null-space">
        <statement>
            <p>Describe the null space of</p>
            <me>
            A=
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1 &amp; 2 &amp; 5\\
            3 &amp; 4 &amp; 0
            \end{bmatrix}.
            </me>

            <p>Solve the homogeneous system <m>Ax=0</m>.</p>

            <me>
            \operatorname{RREF}(A)
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1 &amp; 0 &amp; -10\\
            0 &amp; 1 &amp; \frac{15}{2}
            \end{bmatrix}.
            </me>

            <p>
                Let <m>x_3=t</m>. Then
            </p>

            <me>
            x_1=10t,\qquad
            x_2=-\frac{15}{2}t.
            </me>

            <p>Hence</p>

            <me>
            x
            =
            t
            \begin{bmatrix}
            10\\[4pt]
            -15/2\\[4pt]
            1
            \end{bmatrix}
            .
            </me>

            <p>Therefore</p>

            <me>
            \operatorname{Null}(A)
            =
            \operatorname{span}
            \left\{
            \begin{bmatrix}
            10\\[4pt]
            -15/2\\[4pt]
            1
            \end{bmatrix}
            \right\}.
            </me>
        </statement>
    </example>

    <p>
        Question: How do we check whether
        <m>u\in\operatorname{Null}(A)</m>?
    </p>

    <answer>
        <p>
            Compute <m>Au</m>.
            If <m>Au=0</m>, then
            <m>u\in\operatorname{Null}(A)</m>.
        </p>
    </answer>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-null-subspace">
        <statement>
            <p>
                The null space of an <m>m\times n</m> matrix
                <m>A</m> is a subspace of
                <m>\mathbb{R}^n</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>

    <proof>
        <p>
            To prove this, check the subspace conditions.
        </p>

        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    <em>Zero vector:</em>
                    <m>A0=0</m>, so
                    <m>0\in\operatorname{Null}(A)</m>.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    <em>Closed under addition:</em>
                    If
                    <m>Ax=0</m> and
                    <m>Ay=0</m>, then
                </p>

                <me>
                A(x+y)=Ax+Ay=0+0=0.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Thus
                    <m>x+y\in\operatorname{Null}(A)</m>.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    <em>Closed under scalar multiplication:</em>
                    If
                    <m>Ax=0</m> and
                    <m>c\in\mathbb{R}</m>, then
                </p>

                <me>
                A(cx)=c(Ax)=c(0)=0.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Hence
                    <m>cx\in\operatorname{Null}(A)</m>.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </proof>

    <subsubsection>
        <title>How to Describe the Null Space</title>

        <p>
            The equation <m>Ax=0</m> gives an implicit description of
            <m>\operatorname{Null}(A)</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
            To obtain an explicit description:
        </p>

        <ol>
            <li>Find the RREF <m>U</m> of <m>A</m>.</li>
            <li>Rewrite <m>Ux=0</m> as a linear system.</li>
            <li>Express basic variables in terms of free variables.</li>
            <li>
                Write
                <m>x=c_1v_1+\cdots+c_pv_p</m>,
                where <m>p</m> is the number of free variables.
            </li>
        </ol>

        <p>Then</p>

        <me>
        \operatorname{Null}(A)
        =
        \operatorname{span}\{v_1,\ldots,v_p\}.
        </me>

        <p>
            If there are no free variables, then
        </p>

        <me>
        \operatorname{Null}(A)=\{0\}.
        </me>

        <p>
            The vectors <m>v_1,\ldots,v_p</m> obtained from this process
            are linearly independent.
        </p>
    </subsubsection>

    <definition xml:id="def-col-space">
        <statement>
            <p>
                The <term>column space</term> of an
                <m>m\times n</m> matrix
                <m>A=[a_1\ a_2\ \cdots\ a_n]</m>,
                denoted <m>\operatorname{Col}(A)</m>,
                is the span of all columns of <m>A</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-col-space">
        <statement>
            <p>
                The column space of an
                <m>m\times n</m> matrix is a subspace of
                <m>\mathbb{R}^m</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>

    <proof>
        <p>
            Using the description
            <m>\operatorname{Col}(A)=\{Ax:x\in\mathbb{R}^n\}</m>:
        </p>

        <ul>
            <li>
                If <m>u=Ax</m> and <m>v=Ay</m>,
                then
                <m>u+v=A(x+y)</m>,
                so <m>u+v\in\operatorname{Col}(A)</m>.
            </li>

            <li>
                If <m>u=Ax</m> and <m>c</m> is a scalar,
                then
                <m>cu=A(cx)</m>,
                so <m>cu\in\operatorname{Col}(A)</m>.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </proof>

    <remark>
        <me>
        \operatorname{Col}(A)
        =
        \{\,b : b=Ax
        \text{ for some }
        x\in\mathbb{R}^n\,\}.
        </me>

        <p>
            For the linear transformation
            <m>T(x)=Ax</m>,
            the column space is the range of <m>T</m>.
        </p>
    </remark>

    <example>
        <p>Find the column space of</p>

        <me>
        A=
        \begin{bmatrix}
        1 &amp; 3\\
        2 &amp; 6
        \end{bmatrix}.
        </me>

        <p>
            Since the second column is a multiple of the first,
        </p>

        <me>
        \operatorname{Col}(A)
        =
        \operatorname{span}
        \left\{
        \begin{bmatrix}
        1\\
        2
        \end{bmatrix}
        \right\}.
        </me>
    </example>

    <p>
        Question: Is a matrix with a given column space unique?
    </p>

    <answer>
        <p>No. Many different matrices can have the same column space.</p>
    </answer>

    <p>
        Question: When is <m>\operatorname{Col}(A)=\mathbb{R}^m</m>?
    </p>

    <answer>
        <p>
            Exactly when there is a pivot in every row of <m>A</m>.
        </p>
    </answer>

    <subsubsection>
        <title>Comparison Between Null Space and Column Space</title>

<sidebyside>

        <p><em>Null Space</em></p>
  
        <p><em>Column Space</em></p>
</sidebyside>
<sidebyside>
  
        <ol>
            <li><m>\operatorname{Null}(A)</m> is a subspace of <m>\mathbb{R}^n</m>.</li>
            <li>It takes time to determine explicitly.</li>
            <li>There is no obvious relationship with the entries of <m>A</m>.</li>
            <li><m>v\in\operatorname{Null}(A)</m> iff <m>Av=0</m>.</li>
            <li>Membership is easy to check.</li>
            <li><m>\operatorname{Null}(A)=\{0\}</m> iff <m>Ax=0</m> has only the trivial solution.</li>
            <li><m>\operatorname{Null}(A)=\{0\}</m> iff <m>x\mapsto Ax</m> is one-to-one.</li>
        </ol>


        <ol>
            <li><m>\operatorname{Col}(A)</m> is a subspace of <m>\mathbb{R}^m</m>.</li>
            <li>It is usually easier to identify.</li>
            <li>Each column of <m>A</m> belongs to <m>\operatorname{Col}(A)</m>.</li>
            <li><m>v\in\operatorname{Col}(A)</m> iff <m>Ax=v</m> is consistent.</li>
            <li>Membership generally requires solving a system.</li>
            <li><m>\operatorname{Col}(A)=\mathbb{R}^m</m> iff <m>Ax=b</m> has a solution for every <m>b</m>.</li>
            <li><m>\operatorname{Col}(A)=\mathbb{R}^m</m> iff <m>x\mapsto Ax</m> maps <m>\mathbb{R}^n</m> onto <m>\mathbb{R}^m</m>.</li>
        </ol>

</sidebyside>
    </subsubsection>

    <definition xml:id="def-linear-transformation">
        <statement>
            <p>
                A transformation
                <m>T:V\to W</m>
                is linear if:
            </p>

            <ol>
                <li>
                    <m>T(u+v)=T(u)+T(v)</m>
                    for all <m>u,v\in V</m>.
                </li>

                <li>
                    <m>T(cu)=cT(u)</m>
                    for all <m>u\in V</m> and scalars <m>c</m>.
                </li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <example>
        <p>
            Let
            <m>V</m>
            be the space of functions continuous on
            <m>[a,b]</m> and continuously differentiable on
            <m>(a,b)</m>.
            Define
            <m>T(f)=f'</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
            Then
        </p>

        <me>
        \ker(T)
        =
        \{\text{constant functions}\}.
        </me>
    </example>

    <example>
        <p>
            Let
            <m>V=C[a,b]</m>
            and define
            <m>T(f)=f(a)</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
            Then
        </p>

        <me>
        \ker(T)
        =
        \{f\in C[a,b]:f(a)=0\}.
        </me>
    </example>

</subsection>

    <subsubsection>
      <title>Linear Independence and Bases (VSB2)</title>

      <definition>
        <p>
          Vectors <m>\{v_1,\dots,v_p\}</m> are
          <term>linearly independent</term> if
        </p>

        <me>
        c_1v_1+\cdots+c_pv_p=0
        \Rightarrow
        c_1=\cdots=c_p=0.
        </me>
      </definition>

      <definition>
        <p>
          A set <m>B=\{b_1,\dots,b_p\}</m> is a
          <term>basis</term> for a subspace <m>H</m> if
        </p>

        <ul>
          <li><m>B</m> is linearly independent.</li>
          <li><m>H=\operatorname{span}(B)</m>.</li>
        </ul>
      </definition>

      <subsubsection>
        <title>Reducing a Spanning Set to a Basis</title>

        <algorithm>
          <p>
            Start with a spanning set and remove any vector that is a linear
            combination of earlier vectors. Continue until no vector is
            redundant. The resulting set is a basis.
          </p>
        </algorithm>

        <example>
          <title>Abstract Vector Space Basis</title>

          <p>
            Let
            <m>
            V=\operatorname{span}
            \{(1,1,0,0),(2,1,1,0),(1,0,1,1)\}
            \subset \mathbb R^4.
            </m>
          </p>

          <me>
          A=
          \begin{bmatrix}
          1 &amp;2 &amp;1\\
          1 &amp;1 &amp;0\\
          0 &amp;1 &amp;1\\
          0 &amp;0 &amp;1
          \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>

          <p>
            Row reduction shows pivots in every column, so all three vectors
            are linearly independent and form a basis of <m>V</m>.
          </p>
        </example>
      </subsubsection>
    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection>
      <title>Coordinate Systems and Change of Basis (VSB3)</title>

      <definition>
        <p>
          Let <m>B=\{b_1,\dots,b_p\}</m> be a basis of <m>V</m>.
          Every vector <m>x\in V</m> can be written uniquely as
        </p>

        <me>
        x=c_1b_1+\cdots+c_pb_p.
        </me>

        <p>
          The coefficient vector
        </p>

        <me>
        [x]_B=
        \begin{bmatrix}
        c_1\\
        \vdots\\
        c_p
        \end{bmatrix}
        </me>

        <p>is called the <term>B-coordinate vector</term>.</p>
      </definition>

      <p>
        <strong>Change of Coordinates</strong>
      </p>

        <p>
          For bases <m>B</m> and <m>C</m> of the same space, there exists a
          unique matrix <m>P_{C\leftarrow B}</m> satisfying
        </p>

        <me>
        [x]_C=P_{C\leftarrow B}[x]_B.
        </me>

        <p>
          The columns of <m>P_{C\leftarrow B}</m> are the
          <m>C</m>-coordinates of the basis vectors in <m>B</m>.
        </p>

        <algorithm>
          <ol>
            <li>
              Construct the augmented matrix
              <m>[\,C \mid B\,]</m>.
            </li>
            <li>
              Row-reduce to obtain
              <m>[\,I \mid P_{C\leftarrow B}\,]</m>.
            </li>
          </ol>
        </algorithm>
    
    </subsubsection>

    <subsection>
      <title>Null Space and Its Basis (VSB4)</title>

      <definition>
        <p>
          For an <m>m\times n</m> matrix <m>A</m>, the
          <term>null space</term> is
        </p>

        <me>
        \operatorname{Null}(A)
        =
        \{x\in\mathbb R^n:Ax=0\}.
        </me>
      </definition>

      <subsubsection>
        <title>How to Compute a Basis for the Null Space</title>

        <ol>
          <li>Compute the RREF of <m>A</m>.</li>
          <li>Solve <m>Ux=0</m>.</li>
          <li>Express the solution using free variables.</li>
          <li>The vectors attached to the free variables form a basis.</li>
        </ol>
      </subsubsection>

      <example>
        <title>Finding a Basis for a Null Space</title>

        <me>
        A=
        \begin{bmatrix}
        1 &amp;2 &amp;3 &amp;1\\
        2 &amp;4 &amp;6 &amp;2\\
        1 &amp;1 &amp;1 &amp;0
        \end{bmatrix}.
        </me>

        <p>
          Row reduction gives
        </p>

        <me>
        \begin{bmatrix}
        1 &amp;0 &amp;-1 &amp;-1\\
        0 &amp;1 &amp;2 &amp;1\\
        0 &amp;0 &amp;0 &amp;0
        \end{bmatrix}.
        </me>

        <p>
          Let <m>x_3=s</m> and <m>x_4=t</m>. Then
        </p>

        <me>
        x=
        s\begin{bmatrix}1\\-2\\1\\0\end{bmatrix}
        +
        t\begin{bmatrix}1\\-1\\0\\1\end{bmatrix}.
        </me>

        <p>
          Therefore
        </p>

        <me>
        \operatorname{Null}(A)
        =
        \operatorname{span}
        \left\{
        \begin{bmatrix}1\\-2\\1\\0\end{bmatrix},
        \begin{bmatrix}1\\-1\\0\\1\end{bmatrix}
        \right\}.
        </me>
      </example>
    </subsection>

    <subsection>
      <title>Column Space and Its Basis (VSB5)</title>

      <definition>
        <p>
          For an <m>m\times n</m> matrix
          <m>A=[a_1\ a_2\ \cdots\ a_n]</m>, the
          <term>column space</term> is
        </p>

        <me>
        \operatorname{Col}(A)
        =
        \operatorname{span}\{a_1,\dots,a_n\}.
        </me>
      </definition>

      <fact>
        <p>
          The pivot columns of <m>A</m>, taken from the original matrix,
          form a basis for <m>\operatorname{Col}(A)</m>.
        </p>
      </fact>

      <example>
        <title>Finding a Basis for a Column Space</title>

        <me>
        A=
        \begin{bmatrix}
        1 &amp;3 &amp;4\\
        2 &amp;6 &amp;8\\
        -1 &amp;-3 &amp;-4
        \end{bmatrix}.
        </me>

        <p>
          There is a pivot in column 1 only. Therefore a basis is
        </p>

        <me>
        \left\{
        \begin{bmatrix}
        1\\
        2\\
        -1
        \end{bmatrix}
        \right\}.
        </me>
      </example>

      <example>
        <title>A Richer Example with Two Pivots</title>

        <me>
        B=
        \begin{bmatrix}
        1 &amp;2 &amp;1\\
        0 &amp;1 &amp;1\\
        1 &amp;3 &amp;2
        \end{bmatrix}
        \rightarrow
        \begin{bmatrix}
        1 &amp;0 &amp;-1\\
        0 &amp;1 &amp;1\\
        0 &amp;0 &amp;0
        \end{bmatrix}.
        </me>

        <p>
          The pivot columns are 1 and 2. Hence a basis for
          <m>\operatorname{Col}(B)</m> is
        </p>

        <me>
        \left\{
        \begin{bmatrix}1\\0\\1\end{bmatrix},
        \begin{bmatrix}2\\1\\3\end{bmatrix}
        \right\}.
        </me>
      </example>
    </subsection>

    <subsection>
      <title>Rank, Dimension, and Quick Tests</title>

      <definition>
        <p>
          The <term>rank</term> of a matrix <m>A</m> is
          <m>\dim(\operatorname{Col}(A))</m>.
        </p>
      </definition>

      <definition>
        <p>
          The <term>row space</term> is the span of the rows of a matrix.
          The nonzero rows of the RREF form a basis for the row space.
        </p>
      </definition>

      <fact>
        <p>
          (Rank–Nullity Theorem)
        </p>

        <me>
        \operatorname{rank}(A)
        +
        \dim(\operatorname{Null}(A))
        =
        n.
        </me>
      </fact>

      <example>
        <p>
          If <m>A</m> is a <m>5\times7</m> matrix and
          <m>\dim(\operatorname{Null}(A))=2</m>, then
        </p>

        <me>
        \operatorname{rank}(A)=7-2=5.
        </me>
      </example>

      <subsubsection>
        <title>Quick Checks</title>

        <ul>
          <li>
            <m>\operatorname{Null}(A)=\{0\}</m> iff there is a pivot in
            every column.
          </li>
          <li>
            <m>\operatorname{Col}(A)=\mathbb R^m</m> iff there is a pivot
            in every row.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </subsubsection>
    </subsection>

    <subsection>
      <title>Appendix: Worked “Is It a Vector Space?” Checklist</title>

      <ol>
        <li>
          <emphasis role="bold">Nonempty:</emphasis>
          Find the zero vector and verify it belongs to the set.
        </li>

        <li>
          <emphasis role="bold">Closure:</emphasis>
          Verify closure under addition and scalar multiplication.
        </li>

        <li>
          <emphasis role="bold">Inherited rules:</emphasis>
          Associativity, commutativity, and distributivity typically follow
          from the real numbers when the operations are standard.
        </li>

        <li>
          <emphasis role="bold">Bad cases:</emphasis>
          Watch for hidden constants that prevent the presence of the zero
          vector.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <example>
        <title>Function Space</title>

        <p>
          Let
          <m>\mathcal F=\{f:\mathbb R\to\mathbb R\}</m>.
          With pointwise operations,
          <m>(f+g)(x)=f(x)+g(x)</m> and
          <m>(cf)(x)=cf(x)</m>,
          all vector-space axioms hold.
        </p>
      </example>

      <example>
        <title>A Set That Is Not a Subspace</title>

        <p>
          Consider
          <m>
          W=\{(x,y)\in\mathbb R^2:x-y=1\}.
          </m>
        </p>

        <p>
          Since <m>(0,0)</m> does not satisfy the equation,
          the zero vector is not in <m>W</m>. Therefore
          <m>W</m> is not a subspace.
        </p>
      </example>

    </subsection>

</section>
